[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next question — the other decision that's hard to walk back.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how long the id outlives the page, and therefore who can read it later. You said "persist," which rules out
  memory-only; the real choice is the lifetime.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and in-tab navigation, dies when the tab closes. Buys persistence across the
    reloads that actually break things, with a bounded exposure window. Costs: a new tab is a fresh login, and the id is
    gone after close. Rules out nothing; widening to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close, browser restart, indefinitely, until something explicitly clears it. Buys "log in
    once." Costs: a stale id can outlive the real session by weeks, so you need explicit invalidation you don't have
    yet, and any XSS on this page reads it forever rather than for one tab. Narrowing later is technically one line but
    behaviorally a regression users notice.
  - [1mCookie[0m — only worth it if the [3mserver[0m needs to see the id on each request. From this app's JS it can't be [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m,
    so it's strictly worse than the storage options for client-side reads.

  Worth being plain about one thing: right now this is a tracking id, not a credential — nothing grants access based on
  it. The moment the backend starts trusting it to identify a caller, its storage lifetime becomes a session lifetime,
  and that's a security decision. I'd rather pick the conservative default now than inherit a long-lived one when that
  day comes.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m,[0m behind a small accessor module rather than direct [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m calls scattered
  around — so the "other forms" you mentioned consume a function, and changing the lifetime later is one edit in one
  place.
